{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Duda'im</b> — sigli (Sanhedrin 99a), and in the language of Ishmael, yasmin; thus I found in the commentary of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 30:14). But this is not correct, for yasmin in Arabic is what our Rabbis call by that name, as we say in the chapter BaMeh Tomnin (Shabbat 50a): \"This costpa de-yasmin is permitted.\" And sigli — they say that they are the same as the plant called kenifsaga, concerning which they said (Berakhot 43a) that one recites over them the blessing Borei Isvei Besamim. But their season is not during the wheat harvest; perhaps he found them there by chance. Now, the fitting view concerning the duda'im is the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 30:14), who translated them as yavruchin. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 72:2) as well, Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba said yavruchin, and they are called yabruch in Arabic. And Rabbi Avraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 30:14) wrote: duda'im — the translator said yavruchin, and so they are called in the language of Ishmael, and they have a pleasant fragrance, as it is written (Song of Songs 7:14), \"The duda'im give forth fragrance,\" and they are in the shape of a human being, for they have the form of a head and hands, and some say that they are effective for conception, but I do not know whether their nature produces this effect — these are his words. But the correct interpretation is that she desired them to refresh herself and to delight in their fragrance, for Rachel was remembered through prayer, not by way of remedies. And Reuben brought the branches of the duda'im or the fruit, which resembles apples and has a pleasant fragrance; but the root, which is the part made in the shape of a head and hands, he did not bring. And it is the root of which people say that it is effective for conception; and if this matter is true, it is by their special property (segullah), not by their nature. But I have not seen this stated in any of the books of medicine that discuss them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The passage citing Rashi appears to conflate two separate comments; Rashi cites both sigli and yasmin. The reference to Sanhedrin 99a for sigli may be a secondary citation; the primary discussion of duda'im appears in Shabbat 50a and Berakhot 43a. 'Costpa' is transliterated from the Aramaic in the Talmudic passage; its precise botanical referent is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:01.724172141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}